WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.720
Hey there. Welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners Episode 24 for April 26, 2026.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:10.180
Let's break down today's coolest AI news so anyone can understand it. Let's go.

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:14.520
So imagine you could describe a short video exactly how you picture it in your head.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:19.500
And instead of spending hours learning complicated editing software or hiring a whole team,

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:25.160
an AI just creates it for you in minutes. That is basically what a Reddit creator tested this week.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:30.560
They tried six different AI video generators that turned simple text prompts or images into

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:35.960
short clips, add talking avatars or turn a full script into finished video automatically.

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:41.320
Think of these tools like having a super smart video in turn who never sleeps and never gets tired.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:46.940
You say something like a cool zoom in on a skateboard trick at sunset and the AI tries to build

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:52.520
the footage. Some tools are lightning fast for quick brainstorming ideas while others focus on

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:57.240
making scenes look realistic. A couple even let you act like a real director by controlling exactly

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:03.040
how the camera moves and how fast the pacing feels. The big lesson from all six tests is that extra

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:07.900
control beats pure randomness when you want something that actually looks professional. Higgs field

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:12.440
felt closest to actually building a video rather than just hoping the AI guesses right.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:18.220
This matters a ton for anyone who creates stuff online, especially teens making TikToks,

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:22.940
YouTube shorts, or school projects. Instead of getting stuck learning expensive programs,

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:26.940
you can knock out a gaming highlight reel or a fake ad for your art club in minutes.

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:32.320
The tester found most tools shine at brainstorming, but if you want the final video to feel intentional

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:35.940
like something a brand would use, you will probably mix two or three tools together.

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:41.280
For you specifically, this could mean easier homework videos, way better TikTok content,

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:46.760
or even starting a small creative side hustle. The control versus randomness lesson is huge because

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:51.060
pure luck based generation often looks cool, but does not match what you pictured in your head.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:54.240
Tools that let you steer the shots feel more like real filmmaking.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:58.780
And you can try this yourself right now with no credit card needed on the free tiers.

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:04.360
Head to runway or peka and type a simple prompt like a friendly robot teaching math in a classroom.

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:09.180
Play with the settings then try the exact same idea on Higgs field to see how much more control

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:13.880
you get over camera movement. Compare the results and you will instantly understand exactly what

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:19.240
the tester learned. Okay, now for my favorite part of the show. Let's do a deep dive into how AI

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:24.180
agents can negotiate deals with each other. You know how when you and your friends trade snacks at lunch,

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:29.240
one person might say I will give you my chips if you give me your cookies. You both try to get the

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:34.060
better deal and sometimes you are really good at spotting what the other person values so you walk

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:40.220
away happier. Now picture that same trading game, but the kids are AI agents which are basically smart

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:46.280
programs they can talk, remember rules and make choices on their own. In a recent experiment by

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:51.900
the team at Anthropic, they built a pretend online marketplace where these AI agents acted as both

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:57.380
buyers and sellers. First, each agent gets a clear goal like buy this item as cheaply as possible

00:02:57.380 --> 00:03:02.640
or sell it for as much as you can. Second, they take turns sending messages back and forth just like

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:07.740
texting each other. Third, the stronger smarter agents are better at reading between the lines,

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:12.860
predicting what the other side really wants and gently steering the conversation toward a deal

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:18.620
that favors them. It is not magic. It is pattern matching on a massive scale learned from seeing

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:24.440
millions of conversations during training. The cooler and slightly scary part is that the humans using

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:29.900
the weaker agents never even noticed they got worse deals and that is basically how AI agent

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:35.000
negotiation works. Not so mysterious once you picture the lunch table, right? All right, let us talk

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:39.280
about some other cool stuff you can try today. First up, imagine telling one AI assistant,

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:45.140
I live in Bahrain and love soccer in a normal chat. Then you open a completely different AI app

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:50.800
later and ask where do I live and it already knows the answer. That is exactly what one developer built

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:56.160
using something called persistent memory which means the AI stores important facts about you in a special

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:01.700
layer that any connected model can quietly check. It works across models like the one from Open AI,

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:08.240
Anthropics, Claude, Google's Gemini and XAI's GROC without you copying and pasting anything.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:13.800
This is exciting because it makes AI feel like a real helpful friend instead of a forgetful stranger

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:19.640
every single new chat. For students, it could mean an AI tutor that remembers your weak subjects across

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:24.900
different apps or for fun. It could remember your favorite game characters so every story you create

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:31.700
stays consistent. You can try a live version right now at the site asksary.com. Just tell one model

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:37.020
something personal like your favorite color or dream job. Then open a fresh chat with a completely

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:42.760
different model on the same site and ask it to recall the fact. Watch how the memory travels between

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:49.100
separate AI's. It feels like magic but it is just smart storage. Next, let us look at watermarking AI

00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:55.020
images with hidden patterns. Open AI seems to be testing a new way to mark images made by their

00:04:55.020 --> 00:05:00.660
tools so people can tell they are AI generated. Instead of an obvious logo, they are adding subtle,

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:07.040
blurry, pixel textures that look almost like a hidden QR code. These patterns can hold extra data

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:12.600
like when the image was made or which account created it. It is cool because it helps fight misinformation

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:19.020
if a wild photo goes viral. We might soon check whether it is real or AI made. For teen creators,

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:25.460
it also means more trust when you share your AI art. Try it yourself in chat GPT's image generator.

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:31.100
Make a detailed picture then zoom way in on any slightly weird textured areas and see if you spot

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:35.460
the structured noise. Compare it to normal photos and you will start noticing the difference.

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:40.500
Time for a few quick bits to wrap things up. The United Arab Emirates announced it wants half of

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:46.060
its government work handled by autonomous AI agents within the next two years. These agents would

00:05:46.060 --> 00:05:51.760
make decisions and complete tasks on their own instead of waiting for human approval on routine stuff.

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:57.200
It is a huge real world test of whether AI can run parts of an actual country.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:03.360
Next, a robot named Ace can watch a ping pong ball's path instantly adjust its racket angle and

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:09.400
hit shots that keep long rallies going against real human players. It is an impressive example of AI

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:14.720
combining fast vision with precise movement. One day robots like this could be training partners

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:20.340
or even competitors in real sports. Finally, Stanford scientists gave a language model a DNA sequence

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:26.080
and asked it to design new viruses. The AI created hundreds of ideas, 16 of which actually worked in

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:31.600
tests and one used a protein never seen in any living thing on earth. The story is a serious reminder

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:37.740
that powerful creative AI needs careful rules. That's it for today. Remember every AI expert started

00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:42.260
exactly where you are right now. If something we talked about today made you curious, go try it.

00:06:42.380 --> 00:06:47.180
That is literally how learning works. Stay curious, keep experimenting and we will see you tomorrow.

